First 23; Get-Content docs/requirements/stakeholder-needs.toml | Select-Object -Skip 301 -First 14; Get-Content docs/requirements/system-requirements.toml | Select-Object -Skip 1143 -First 14; Get-Content project-trajectory/scripts/plan_briefs.py | Select-Object -Skip 304 -First 98'","aggregated_output":"[interface.IF-113]\r\nrationale = \"\"\"The loader's both-ways strictness is load-bearing here rather than merely tidy: a judge's brief whose evidence section is thin reads as an investigation that was run and found nothing, so there is deliberately no partially-filled path across this seam.\"\"\"\r\nowner = \"scripts/prompts\"\r\nrequestors = [\"scripts/adjudicate_brief\", \"scripts/adjudicate_act\"]\r\nchannel = \"call\"\r\ndata = \"ADJUDICATE-AMENDMENT · ADJUDICATE-DISPOSITION · ADJUDICATE-RED-TC · ADJUDICATE-DONE-WHEN · ADJUDICATE-COMBINED · ADJUDICATE-ACT · PROMPTS / SPINE_QUESTIONS\"\r\nversion = \"v1\"\r\nstatus = \"Drafted\"\r\ncomponent = \"CMP-008\"\r\nnotes = \"\"\"intra-component: both endpoints are CMP-008 under the narrow-waist partition, which puts the coordinator and the prompt loader in one unit, so the architecture rule no longer demands this row; it stands as the declared consumption of the loader contract, whose provider side is IF-097. Sibling of IF-099 (the session engine's own use of the same loader): the difference is that a failed fill here HOLDS the work item for a human rather than degrading, because the alternative brief would be the implementer's.\"\"\"\r\n\r\n[interface.IF-114]\r\nrationale = \"\"\"Read through the carrier rather than a path with a suffix because the brief must assemble identically either side of the carrier cutover. Every listed cell is required rather than dash-filled, since a dash in an evidence listing reads as \"checked, not applicable\" when the truth is \"the registry never said\".\"\"\"\r\nowner = \"scripts/spine_carrier\"\r\nrequestors = [\"scripts/adjudicate_brief\"]\r\nchannel = \"call\"\r\ndata = \"spine_carrier.load over the TC, SR and LLR tiers; every listed cell required, read-only\"\r\ncarried_by = \"IF-102\"\r\nversion = \"v1\"\r\nstatus = \"Drafted\"\r\ncomponent = \"CMP-008\"\r\nnotes = \"\"\"the cross-component edge CMP-008 -> CMP-006, declared per the architecture rule. Read-only: this module never writes a registry - the flip a verdict may earn is the mechanical tool's act, which is also what the briefs themselves tell the session.\"\"\"\r\n\r\n[interface.IF-115]\r\nowner = \"scripts/adjudicate_brief\"\r\nrequestors = [\"scripts/agent_loop\"]\r\nchannel = \"call\"\r\ndata = \"compose(...) -> text | reason; record_outcome(...) -> (accepted | failed, why | None)\"\r\nversion = \"v1\"\r\nstatus = \"Drafted\"\r\ncomponent = \"CMP-008\"\r\nnotes = \"intra-component (both CMP-008), declared because the fail-closed obligation lives on the CALLER and would otherwise be recorded nowhere. `ROUTED` names all eight shipped briefs with an assembler; `conflict` is retired, with its missing derivation named in the module header. The judging composer derives no approval-registry token; the act brief joins the retained identity of actable classes.\"\r\n\r\n[interface.IF-117]\r\nowner = \"scripts/gen_arch_map\"\r\nrequestors = [\"scripts/check_doc_refs\"]\r\nchannel = \"call\"\r\ndata = \"module_bindings(tree) -> the module-scope names plus class-level def names\"\r\nversion = \"v1\"\r\nstatus = \"Drafted\"\r\ncomponent = \"CMP-007\"\r\n\r\n[interface.IF-118]\r\nrationale = \"\"\"The fail-closed half is what this seam is FOR: read directly, a registry that existed and would not parse came back as no rows, and this view then published \"0 pending decisions\" - which on a decision queue reads as \"nothing is waiting on you\".\"\"\"\r\n[interface.IF-133]\r\nowner = \"scripts/spine_carrier\"\r\nrequestors = [\"scripts/hats\"]\r\nchannel = \"call\"\r\ndata = \"spine_carrier.load_needs, load, resolve, stem, folded, NEED_CARRIERS; absent registry [] and VACUOUS, unreadable carrier SystemExit\"\r\ncarried_by = \"IF-102\"\r\nversion = \"v1\"\r\nstatus = \"Drafted\"\r\ncomponent = \"CMP-008\"\r\nnotes = \"\"\"cross-component crossing declared per the architecture rule: this module is CMP-008 (LLR-168) and spine_carrier is CMP-006 (LLR-166). The IF-118/119/120/122 carrier-consumption shape. The ROSTER beside it is read with tomllib directly and the two reads do not contradict: a roster row is keyed by name, is no tier and traces nowhere, while a need row is a spine row whose vocabulary - which carrier is live, what an unreadable registry means - has one home. The consumer-side contract test is tests/test_hats.py, which drives both halves this row states: an unreadable needs registry refuses instead of auditing as zero needs, and a legacy .md registry still reaches the worksheet.\"\"\"\r\n\r\n[interface.IF-134]\r\nowner = \"hooks/pre-commit\"\r\nconsumers = [\"external:git\"]\r\nchannel = \"exit-code\"\r\ndata = \"exit 0 admits the commit; nonzero refuses it\"\r\nversion = \"v1\"\r\nstatus = \"Drafted\"\r\ncomponent = \"CMP-007\"\r\ninterface_from_external = \"B-01\"\r\nbridged_by = [\"DA-003\", \"DA-004\"]\r\ninterface_to_external = \"B-04\"\r\nnotes = \"\"\"The FACING row for the hook floor, and it is deliberately separate from the rows that model the floor's COMPOSITION. Those state which script the hook runs - an internal call between two in-tree endpoints - while the crossing is the contract git holds the hook to: git invokes, the exit code admits or refuses. A tie-back on an internal-call row would claim a realization that row's own contract does not state, so the gap was never a missing surface but a missing FACING. The bypass rides the contract rather than hiding behind it: what discharges `no unchecked write enters governed state` is this seam PAIRED with a re-run of the same bar away from the bypass, never this seam alone.\"\"\"\r\n[need.SN-036]\r\nstatus = \"Approved\"\r\nstakeholder_refs = [\"STK-01\"]\r\npriority = \"S\"\r\nneed = \"\"\"**Scope: template (adopters + this repo).** When a stakeholder's need is turned into detailed requirements, it is examined from every relevant expert and downstream-user perspective so important failure cases are not lost.\"\"\"\r\nwhy = \"\"\"A decomposition produced from one perspective can omit security, operations, accessibility or first-use failures while still appearing complete. The omission remains invisible unless the review records which relevant perspectives were considered and what each contributed.\"\"\"\r\nacceptance = \"\"\"Each decomposition has a machine-readable record of the declared perspectives, the applicability decision for each perspective, and the requirements or explicit no-finding result produced from it. A check reports any applicable declared perspective missing from that record. The record proves coverage and provenance; an independent review judges whether the resulting requirements are adequate.\"\"\"\r\n\r\n[need.SN-037]\r\nstatus = \"Approved\"\r\nstakeholder_refs = [\"STK-03\"]\r\npriority = \"M\"\r\nneed = \"\"\"**Scope: template (adopters + this repo).** A stakeholder can see where each promised system behavior enters or leaves the system, and the architecture changes with those promises instead of being invented afterward.\"\"\"\r\nwhy = \"\"\"A requirement whose inputs and outputs cannot be located at a declared system boundary cannot be verified or assigned to a responsible component. Updating requirements and architecture separately also leaves periods in which each tells a different story.\"\"\"\r\n[requirement.SR-186]\r\ntitle = \"Proportionate requirement decomposition\"\r\nsn_refs = [\"SN-012\"]\r\nboundary_refs = [\"B-05\"]\r\nhat_refs = [\"MAINTAINER\", \"PRODUCT-FITNESS\"]\r\nrequirement = \"The delivered requirements process shall require each additional child within a required tier to carry an independent decision or verification purpose, and record the stopping decision in the scoped decomposition record, while retaining the required SN-to-SR-to-LLR-to-TC tiers and linking real verification to the existing obligation.\"\r\nrationale = \"Carries SN-012's unowned granularity clause as a process requirement on what the delivered process requires and records. A child that only paraphrases a parent or duplicates another child's verification adds review and maintenance cost without an independent decision. The process records the stopping decision within the required spine; this row does not guarantee adopter behavior beyond that record, and it never permits omitting a required SN→SR→LLR→TC tier or replacing real verification with a count. It does not impose a row-count cap, deletion quota, or new machine gate. Existing process doctrine and the spine-authoring adjudicator questions are the source of the judgment, while mechanism-specific evidence remains with its current SR.\"\r\nacceptance_criteria = \"For a reviewed decomposition, each additional child within a required tier has an independent decision or verification purpose traceable to its parent; when further splitting would only paraphrase or duplicate, the existing scoped decomposition/review record states why the process stops at that independent-value boundary.\"\r\npriority = \"S\"\r\nverification = \"Inspection\"\r\nstatus = \"Approved\"\r\nphase = 5\r\ncoincident = \"\"\"The need asks for a decomposition that stays proportionate; each additional child's recorded purpose, and the recorded stopping decision, is that outcome, judged by review.\"\"\"\r\naspect = \"process\"\r\ndef _parent_need_ids(root, row):\r\n    \"\"\"Exact SN parents reached from explicit SR-Refs and canonical SpecRef.\"\"\"\r\n    sr_refs = _refs(row.get(\"SR-Refs\"))\r\n    need_ids = {}\r\n    if sr_refs:\r\n        if spine_carrier.resolve(root / SR_CSV) is None:\r\n            raise HatsError(\r\n                \"cannot resolve declared SR-Refs {}: system-requirements carrier \"\r\n                \"is absent\".format(\", \".join(sr_refs))\r\n            )\r\n        sr_rows = {\r\n            str(sr.get(\"SR-ID\") or \"\"): sr\r\n            for sr in spine_carrier.load(root / SR_CSV, \"SR-ID\", keep_examples=False)\r\n        }\r\n        missing_srs = [ref for ref in sr_refs if ref not in sr_rows]\r\n        if missing_srs:\r\n            raise HatsError(\r\n                \"cannot resolve declared SR-Refs: unknown SR id(s) \"\r\n                + \", \".join(missing_srs)\r\n            )\r\n        for sr_id in sr_refs:\r\n            for need_id in _refs(sr_rows[sr_id].get(\"SN-Refs\")):\r\n                need_ids.setdefault(need_id, None)\r\n\r\n    spec = str(row.get(\"SpecRef\") or \"\").strip()\r\n    spec_need = _canonical_need_ref(spec)\r\n    if spec_need:\r\n        need_ids.setdefault(spec_need, None)\r\n    return need_ids\r\n\r\n\r\ndef _parent_need_contexts(root, row, work_context):\r\n    \"\"\"One WI+SN context per parent; declared missing inputs refuse.\"\"\"\r\n    need_ids = _parent_need_ids(root, row)\r\n    if not need_ids:\r\n        return []\r\n    if spine_carrier.resolve(root / NEEDS_REL, spine_carrier.NEED_CARRIERS) is None:\r\n        raise HatsError(\r\n            \"cannot resolve declared stakeholder-need references {}: \"\r\n            \"stakeholder-needs carrier is absent\".format(\", \".join(need_ids))\r\n        )\r\n    needs = {\r\n        str(need.get(\"id\") or \"\"): need\r\n        for need in spine_carrier.load_needs(root / NEEDS_REL)\r\n    }\r\n    missing_needs = [need_id for need_id in need_ids if need_id not in needs]\r\n    if missing_needs:\r\n        raise HatsError(\r\n            \"cannot resolve declared stakeholder-need reference(s): unknown \"\r\n            \"SN id(s) {}\".format(\", \".join(missing_needs))\r\n        )\r\n    return [_hat_parent_context(work_context, needs[need_id]) for need_id in need_ids]\r\n\r\n\r\ndef hat_surface_for_work_item(root, row):\r\n    \"\"\"Hat surface for a WI and each exact parent, without merging parents.\"\"\"\r\n    root = Path(root)\r\n    roster = hats_roster.load(root)\r\n    if not roster:  # absent/empty roster is the declared opt-out\r\n        return {HAT_QUESTIONS_SLOT: hats_roster.brief_block(roster)}\r\n    work_context = hats_roster.context_from_work_item(row)\r\n    contexts = [work_context] + _parent_need_contexts(root, row, work_context)\r\n    selected = {\r\n        hat[\"name\"]\r\n        for context in contexts\r\n        for hat in hats_roster.applicable(roster, context)\r\n    }\r\n\r\n    chosen = [hat for hat in roster if hat[\"name\"] in selected]\r\n    return {HAT_QUESTIONS_SLOT: hats_roster.brief_block(chosen)}\r\n\r\n\r\n# --- strict slot fill ---------------------------------------------------------\r\ndef _placeholders(text):\r\n    \"\"\"The set of `{{NAME}}` placeholder names present in `text`.\"\"\"\r\n    return set(PLACEHOLDER_RE.findall(text))\r\n\r\n\r\ndef declares_slot(template_text, name):\r\n    \"\"\"Whether `template_text` declares the `{{name}}` placeholder.\r\n\r\n    The guard for an OPTIONAL slot. `assemble` rejects a slot key the template\r\n    does not declare, so a caller adding a slot to the shipped template must\r\n    ask before filling it — otherwise every operator override written against\r\n    the older template stops composing at all.\"\"\"\r\n    return name in _placeholders(template_text)\r\n\r\n\r\ndef assemble(hat, slots, template_text):\r\n    \"\"\"Strict slot-fill of the `{{NAME}}` placeholders in `template_text`.\r\n\r\n    Every provided slot key must name a placeholder present in the template\r\n    (unknown keys raise ValueError); after the fill, ANY placeholder left\r\n    unprovided raises ValueError naming it — a half-filled brief (a hole where a\r\n    redacted input belongs) never reaches a session. `hat` labels the error\r\n    context. Returns the filled text.\r\n\r\n    Note the unfilled check is computed from the template's own placeholder set,\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
